#!/bin/sh
#
# script/runner-container: boot the suite over and over inside an approximation of the CI runner,
# because the failure being hunted has never once appeared outside one.
#
#   script/runner-container                       50 riscv64 boots, the default hunt
#   script/runner-container -n 5                  5 boots
#   script/runner-container -n 20 --arch aarch64  a different leg
#   script/runner-container --build-only          build the image and warm the cache, run nothing
#   script/runner-container --shell               a shell in the container, cache warm
#   script/runner-container --help
#
# **What it is for.** `inbound check (riscv64)` has gone red on CI three times with nothing anybody
# could name, at roughly one boot in six, and fifteen boots across three developer lanes on macOS
# have produced zero failures. notes/net.md's inbound BUGS section draws the conclusion: this is an
# emulator-timing failure, CI is `ubuntu-24.04-arm`, and **a reproduction attempt that is not on that
# runner class is not a reproduction attempt.** So the instrument is not another loop on the laptop.
# It is the runner class, brought to the laptop.
#
# **Why podman and not a second machine.** `ubuntu-24.04-arm` is aarch64 and so is this laptop, so
# the container runs NATIVE: no second layer of emulation between the host and the QEMU under
# examination, which for a timing measurement is the whole reason a container is admissible here at
# all. On an x86 developer machine this script would be measuring qemu-user and would be worthless.
#
# **What it matches, and this list is the argument that the run means anything:**
#
#   - the distribution and release, ubuntu 24.04 aarch64, hence its libc, its libslirp and its
#     scheduler tick;
#   - the emulator, built from `.qemu-version`'s pin by `script/ci-qemu`, THE SAME SCRIPT the
#     workflow runs, into the same `~/.cache/nife-qemu` prefix an actions/cache would restore;
#   - the provisioning, `script/bootstrap`, again the workflow's own;
#   - the core count. `--cpuset-cpus` and not `--cpus`, and the difference is the point: `--cpus` is
#     a CFS quota and leaves `nproc` reading the whole machine, so the guest would still be scheduled
#     across every core, throttled. A cpuset actually narrows `sched_getaffinity`, so TCG's threads
#     see four processors the way the runner's do. CI's own load diagnostic reports 4.
#
# **What it does NOT match, stated here rather than discovered later**, because the bar is "close
# enough that a one-in-six failure appears" and not "identical":
#
#   - **the kernel.** Podman on macOS is a Linux VM (applehv), so the container shares THAT kernel,
#     not GitHub's. Its version, its scheduler and its virtio timer sources are the VM's.
#   - **nested virtualisation.** The runner is a VM on GitHub's fabric; this is a container in a VM
#     on a laptop that is also running a browser. Scheduling jitter, which is the family this bug is
#     in, is exactly what differs.
#   - **the host TCP stack.** The connections the prober makes are host-loopback, and they are
#     terminated by the podman VM's Linux stack rather than by macOS's, which is a match; but the
#     path from the `podman run` process to it is not the runner's.
#   - **memory.** A GitHub `ubuntu-24.04-arm` runner has 16 GiB. The podman VM ships with 4 and this
#     script does not change it, because a machine's shape is the operator's; `--build-only` will
#     tell you if yours is too small to build QEMU.
#   - **the image.** ubuntu:24.04, not a `runner-images` build. See the Containerfile.
#
# So a reproduction here is strong evidence and a clean run is weak evidence. That asymmetry is why
# the failure to reproduce still has to be reported as a result: it says the failure depends on
# something in the list above, which narrows where the next person looks.
#
# **Nothing here writes into the repository.** The tree is bind-mounted READ-ONLY and cloned inside
# the container; the clone, the build products and the toolchains live in a named podman volume.
# A lane's worktree cannot be touched by a run, and neither can the account-global `nife-dev`
# toolchain link, because the container has a `$HOME` of its own. That last one is not tidiness: on
# this project `script/test` takes that link account-wide, and a fifty-boot loop that held it for
# three hours would break every other lane on the machine.
#
# **QEMU cannot leak out of a run.** The emulators live in the container's PID namespace inside the
# podman VM, so the host's `pgrep qemu` never sees them and `--rm` on a container that exits takes
# every one of them with it. That is a genuinely better containment than the bounded-run discipline
# the rest of this tree needs, and it is worth knowing before someone adds a `qemu-bounded.sh` here.
#
# Name: **provisional**, minted by the `ci/runner-container` lane on 2026-08-19 and not yet put to
# calef. It names what the thing is (a container that stands in for the CI runner) rather than what
# it was built for, on `repeat-under-load`'s reasoning: the instrument outlives this hunt, and a
# name like `flake-hunt` would be naming a hope. Hyphenated per milestone 63's rule for this
# directory. Considered and not taken: `ci-container` (reads as "the container CI uses", which it is
# not: CI has no container), and `runner-repro` (names an outcome the script cannot promise, which
# is the same objection `flake-hunt` failed on).

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

IMAGE=nife-runner-container
VOLUME=nife-runner-container-cache
CPUSET=0-3
RUNS=50
LEG=riscv64
MODE=hunt

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        -n) RUNS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --arch) LEG="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --build-only) MODE=build; shift ;;
        --shell) MODE=shell; shift ;;
        --inside) MODE=inside; shift ;;
        -h|--help) sed -n '2,10p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
        *)
            echo "runner-container: unknown option '$1'" >&2
            exit 2
            ;;
    esac
done

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The half that runs INSIDE the container. It is in this file rather than in a second one so that
# `script/lint` shellchecks it, and so that a reader sees the whole experiment in one place; the
# container reaches it through the read-only mount of the tree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ "$MODE" = inside ]; then
    ref="${NIFE_RUNNER_REF:?the host half sets this}"

    # A fresh clone every time, so no run can be contaminated by the last one's working tree, with
    # `target/` symlinked onto the volume so the several minutes of cargo build are paid once. The
    # tree's own code reaches build products through the relative path `target/`, so a symlink is
    # the one way to persist them without teaching xtask about a second location.
    echo "==> cloning $ref from the read-only mount"
    rm -rf /work
    git clone --quiet --no-hardlinks --branch "$ref" /src /work
    mkdir -p /cache/target
    ln -s /cache/target /work/target
    cd /work
    echo "==> under test: $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) on $ref"

    # The workflow's own two steps, in the workflow's own order. `ci-qemu` is idempotent and exits in
    # milliseconds once the volume holds the pinned build, which is what makes the second run of this
    # script cheap.
    script/ci-qemu
    script/bootstrap

    echo "==> $(nproc) processors visible, $(qemu-system-riscv64 --version | head -1)"

    # `-s 0`: NO induced load. That is deliberate and it is the opposite of what
    # `script/repeat-under-load` is normally for. Both CI observations of this failure had a load
    # average around 1 on four cores, so contention is already ruled out as the explanation; adding
    # spinners would be manufacturing a different bug. What is wanted is fifty quiet boots on the
    # runner's shape, which is the one variable that has never been tried.
    script/repeat-under-load -n "$NIFE_RUNNER_RUNS" -s 0 -- --arch "$NIFE_RUNNER_LEG" || true

    # The logs are the deliverable, so they leave the container even if the loop died partway.
    mkdir -p /logs
    cp -R target/acceptance/. /logs/ 2>/dev/null || true
    echo "==> logs copied to the host"
    exit 0
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The half that runs on the host.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
command -v podman >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    echo "runner-container: podman is not on PATH; see the Containerfile for what this needs." >&2
    exit 1
}

# The clone source is the MAIN checkout, not this worktree: a worktree's `.git` is a file pointing
# into the shared object store, so a container that mounted only the worktree could not clone from
# it. `--git-common-dir` is the one command that answers this correctly from either.
common="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)"
case "$common" in
    /*) ;;
    *) common="$(pwd)/$common" ;;
esac
src="$(dirname "$common")"
ref="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"

logs="$(pwd)/target/runner-container-logs"
mkdir -p "$logs"

echo "==> building $IMAGE"
podman build -t "$IMAGE" -f .github/runner-container/Containerfile .github/runner-container

podman volume create "$VOLUME" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

# The mounts, and each one is a decision:
#   /src      read-only, and it is the whole point of the isolation: the run cannot write into the
#             repository or into any lane's worktree, which is the hazard a fifty-boot loop
#             otherwise carries. `:ro` is checked by the kernel, not by the script's intentions.
#   /harness  read-only, THIS worktree, and it exists for one reason: /src is a repository whose
#             working tree is checked out at whatever branch its owner is on, so the copy of this
#             script inside it is whatever that branch has, which on `main` is nothing at all. The
#             container has to be handed the harness separately from the tree it clones.
#   /cache    the volume: the built QEMU, the toolchains, the cargo registry, the clone's `target/`.
#   /logs     the only writable path that leads back to the host, and it is under `target/`, which
#             is already ignored.
# `:z` is deliberately absent: this is not SELinux-labelled storage on macOS, and relabelling a
# read-only mount of somebody's repository is not something a hunt script should do.
run_container() {
    podman run --rm -i \
        --cpuset-cpus "$CPUSET" \
        -v "$src:/src:ro" \
        -v "$(pwd):/harness:ro" \
        -v "$VOLUME:/cache" \
        -v "$logs:/logs" \
        -e NIFE_RUNNER_REF="$ref" \
        -e NIFE_RUNNER_RUNS="$1" \
        -e NIFE_RUNNER_LEG="$LEG" \
        "$IMAGE" "${2:-/harness/script/runner-container}" "${3:---inside}"
}

case "$MODE" in
    build)
        # Zero boots. `script/repeat-under-load -n 0` runs nothing, so the same one code path
        # provisions the volume and the hunt uses it; a second copy of the clone-and-bootstrap
        # sequence here would be a thing to keep in agreement for no gain.
        echo "==> warming the cache (QEMU from source on a cold volume; ~15 minutes)"
        run_container 0
        ;;
    shell)
        run_container 0 /bin/bash -i
        ;;
    hunt)
        echo "==> $RUNS boots of the $LEG leg on $CPUSET, logs in $logs"
        run_container "$RUNS"
        ;;
esac
